  <result aggregation-status="0" type="3">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Contribute to the improvement of Socio-Economic Welfare of Women in Cross-Border Trade Between DRC and Rwanda</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Generally, the environment for WICBTs has been improved in terms of safety and security at the border posts.  Currently, cases of harassment were reduced and WICBTs have confidence to report any GBV issues they face at the border posts and elsewhere they do business in both countries. Border officials increased awareness on women&#x2019;s rights, GBV prevention and fully engaged to prevent any harassment towards WICBTs .
The wellbeing of women is improved through understanding and utilization of available SRHR services. WICBTs and their husbands have knowledge on SRHR, Comprehensive sexuality, and birth control. Linking women and their husbands/ partners to health facilities for health-related services accessibility and utilization is vital. In addition, WICBTs and their husbands/ partners with enhanced skills in entrepreneurship and financial literacy, linked to financial institution for their business development and access to financial benefits eventually increase their income and social economic welfare. Saving was one of the biggest achievement and promising sustainability mechanism of the project intervention. During a period of one year, the average of saving per cooperative eight hundred thousand Rwandan Francs which indicates the level of understanding increased for the WICBTs. Working in cooperatives also motivated women to harmonize their business and be able to send the products to the other side of the border during COVID 19 restrictions to cross borders.</narrative>
   </description>
   <document-link format="APPLICATION/1D-INTERLEAVED-PARITYFEC" url="https://iatipublisher-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/document-link/98/ANNUAL-NARRATIVE-REPORT-FOR-2021-AND-ANNUAL-PLAN-FOR-2022-(EWICBT-PROJECT)-20220822120812.pdf">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">P
rior to EWICBT Project, the EKN Great Lakes programme had established a platform where organizations implementing projects related to SRHR meet to share information about their projects, identified best practices and challenges. The purpose of platform is to create synergies and collaborations where organizations or projects activities can be complementary. As a result, PFTH and iPeace have joined the platform. The synergy developed by this platform has been stronger in DRC where iPeace and Care International are collaborating in providing access to SRHR services to WICBTs in health facilities supported by Care International. Collaboration is also underway between iPeace and Radio la Benevolencija to raise awareness about the rights and obligations of WICBTs.</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project&#x2019;s activities are implemented in a very volatile post-conflict area affected by a decade of interstate conflict where North Kivu and South Kivu were contested between the military influences of Rwanda and DRC. Although today the situation is relatively calm, it must be acknowledged that the aftermath of this sad period still affects the relationship of people and communities at the grassroots level. The EWICBT project is aware of this and has developed a do-no-harm approach to avoid that project activities may be a factor that awaken the daemons of conflict. It is in this regard that a workshop was organized by the EKN to empower staff working in Dutch-funded projects with knowledge and skills about conflict sensitivity.</narrative>
    </description>
